        <ArticleTitle>Aspirin resistance and cardiovascular outcomes in diabetes mellitus</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>456-465</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1161/ATVBAHA.123.318456</ELocationID>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND">Aspirin resistance is associated with adverse cardiovascular outcomes in patients with diabetes mellitus.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS">We studied 500 diabetic patients on aspirin therapy. Platelet function was measured by VerifyNow assay. COX-1 and COX-2 expression was analyzed.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS">Aspirin resistance (ARU > 550) was found in 28% of patients. High COX-1 expression correlated with resistance (p&lt;0.001). EGFR signaling was upregulated in resistant patients.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS">COX-1 overexpression and EGFR pathway activation contribute to aspirin resistance in diabetes.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
