        <ArticleTitle>Drug repurposing for Alzheimer's disease: targeting amyloid beta and tau</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>123-140</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41573-023-00892-1</ELocationID>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND">Alzheimer's disease (AD) lacks disease-modifying treatments. Repurposing approved drugs offers a faster path to clinic.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="APPROACH">We screened 1,200 approved compounds for amyloid beta (Aβ) aggregation inhibition. BACE1 and APP processing were assessed in neuronal cultures.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="FINDINGS">Sildenafil (CHEMBL132) reduced Aβ42 by 45% via PDE5A inhibition. Metformin (CHEMBL1100) decreased tau phosphorylation through AMPK activation. Both compounds cross the blood-brain barrier.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="IMPLICATIONS">PDE5A and AMPK represent promising targets for AD drug repurposing.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
