        <ArticleTitle>COX-2 selective inhibitors and cardiovascular risk: a mechanistic review</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>2100-2115</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acs.jmedchem.3c01234</ELocationID>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText>COX-2 selective inhibitors (coxibs) were developed to reduce gastrointestinal toxicity of traditional NSAIDs. However, rofecoxib (CHEMBL152) withdrawal highlighted cardiovascular risks. Celecoxib (CHEMBL118) shows dose-dependent risk. The mechanism involves prostacyclin (PGI2) suppression without affecting thromboxane A2, creating prothrombotic state. TP53 and EGFR pathways modulate this effect.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
