        <ArticleTitle>Metformin activates AMPK in neurons: implications for neurodegenerative diseases</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>45-56</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1124/molpharm.123.000456</ELocationID>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND">Metformin (CHEMBL1100) is a first-line diabetes drug with emerging neuroprotective effects.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS">Primary cortical neurons treated with metformin. AMPK phosphorylation, mTOR signaling, and autophagy markers measured.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS">Metformin (10 μM) increased p-AMPK by 3.2-fold, inhibited mTOR, and enhanced autophagy. Effects blocked by Compound C (AMPK inhibitor). APP processing shifted away from amyloidogenic pathway.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS">Metformin's neuroprotection is AMPK-dependent and reduces amyloidogenic processing.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
