        <ArticleTitle>Thalidomide for multiple myeloma: from teratogen to targeted therapy</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination>
          <MedlinePgn>890-898</MedlinePgn>
        </Pagination>
        <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/eurheartj/ehae123</ELocationID>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="HISTORICAL CONTEXT">Thalidomide (CHEMBL382) was withdrawn in 1961 for teratogenicity. Rediscovered for leprosy and multiple myeloma.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="MECHANISM">Thalidomide binds CRBN (cereblon), modulating E3 ubiquitin ligase activity. This degrades IKZF1/3 transcription factors, killing myeloma cells. Also inhibits TNF-alpha and angiogenesis via VEGF suppression.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL USE">Now standard in multiple myeloma regimens. Pomalidomide (CHEMBL2103798) and lenalidomide (CHEMBL1198) are derivatives with improved safety.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
